      "content": "International organizations like the UN are structurally designed to prioritize multilateral consensus over specific national interests, often allowing dominant powers or regional blocs to veto actions or dilute resolutions that threaten smaller states. Without a true executive enforcement mechanism, these bodies rely entirely on voluntary compliance, which frequently evaporates when a member state becomes uncooperative or when global strategic priorities clash with local sovereignty. Consequently, nations often find their long-term security and autonomy compromised as global governance mechanisms struggle to balance competing geopolitical interests effectively.",
